  "evidence": "Kubernetes docs: \"Kubernetes does not have objects which represent normal user accounts. Normal users cannot be added to a cluster through an API call.\"",
  "notes": "Open-source container orchestrator with no paid tiers. There is nothing to provision: Kubernetes keeps no user accounts and expects an outside service (an IdP, certificates) to manage users; it authenticates them per request with client certificates or OIDC/JWT tokens and authorizes them through RBAC bindings to user and group names. So there is no SCIM and no user-management API, and no JIT accounts are created. Managed services (EKS, GKE, AKS, OpenShift) add their own identity layers and are out of scope for this row.",
  "confidence": "medium",
